\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 1: Understand the Existing Opportunity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Read the results from:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Phase 2\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 and:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Phase 3\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Understand:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
What does the existing app do?\
\
What problem does it solve?\
\
Who uses it?\
\
What complaints do users have?\
\
What features do users like?\
\
What competitors exist?\
\
What alternatives exist?\
\
Why might users pay?\
\
Why might the business succeed?\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Do not repeat all previous research.\
Use the existing research to make design decisions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 2: Identify the Core Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is one of the most important steps.\
Determine:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 What problem should our product actually solve?
\f1\b0 \
Do not simply say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
"We should build a better version of App X."\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Instead, identify the underlying problem.\
For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Existing App:\
\
Fishing Tracker\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The real problem might be:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
"I want to quickly remember and analyze my fishing trips."\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 That is more useful than:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
"We need to build a fishing app."\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
core_problem\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Keep it concise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 3: Choose the Target User\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Do not try to build for everyone.\
Determine the most promising user.\
Examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Casual fishermen\
\
Serious hobbyists\
\
Professional guides\
\
Competitive anglers\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 These may all use fishing software.\
But they may want very different things.\
Select the user group that appears:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Most likely to have the problem\
\
Most dissatisfied with existing solutions\
\
Most likely to use a better solution\
\
Most likely to pay\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
target_user\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 4: Determine Product Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Determine the basic strategy for the product.\
Possible strategies include:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
SIMPLER\
\
CHEAPER\
\
MORE_SPECIALIZED\
\
BETTER_WORKFLOW\
\
BETTER_USER_EXPERIENCE\
\
DIFFERENT_AUDIENCE\
\
SOLVE_MISSING_PROBLEM\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The product may use more than one strategy.\
Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
SIMPLER\
+\
MORE_SPECIALIZED\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Existing fishing apps attempt to provide:\
\
Maps\
\
Weather\
\
Social networking\
\
Species databases\
\
Fishing reports\
\
Community features\
\
Gear stores\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The new product might instead focus on:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Quickly logging fishing trips\
\
Tracking catches\
\
Photos\
\
Personal statistics\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The strategy is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
SIMPLER\
\
MORE_SPECIALIZED\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
product_strategy\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 5: Define Differentiation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Answer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Why would someone choose this product?
\f1\b0 \
Use the research from Phase 2.\
Look for:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Repeated complaints\
\
Missing features\
\
Bad workflows\
\
Overly complicated competitors\
\
Expensive pricing\
\
Neglected user groups\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Create a concise statement.\
Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
A fast and simple fishing journal designed specifically\
for individual anglers who want to record and analyze\
their own fishing history without advertisements,\
social networking, or unnecessary features.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
differentiation\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The differentiation should be meaningful.\
Do not use:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
"Better UI"\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 by itself.\
That is not a product strategy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 6: Define Must-Have Features\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Determine the smallest set of features required to solve the core problem.\
Ask:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 If we removed this feature, would the product still solve the main problem?
\f1\b0 \
If yes:\
It probably does not belong in the MVP.\
Store the features as JSON.\
Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
[\
  "Create fishing trip",\
  "Record catch",\
  "Add species",\
  "Add location",\
  "Attach photo",\
  "View personal catch history"\
]\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
must_have_features\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Keep this list small.\
A reasonable MVP should generally have:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
3\'9610 core features\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Not:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
47 features\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 7: Define Nice-to-Have Features\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 These are useful features that should 
\f0\b not be required for the first MVP
\f1\b0 .\
Examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
Cloud synchronization\
\
Advanced statistics\
\
Social features\
\
Sharing\
\
AI recommendations\
\
Apple Watch integration\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store as JSON:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
nice_to_have_features\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 These are intentionally excluded from the first build.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 8: Define Excluded Features\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is important.\
Explicitly identify things the product should 
\f0\b not do
\f1\b0 .\
Existing apps often become bloated because they try to solve every possible problem.\
Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
[\
  "Social network",\
  "Public fishing maps",\
  "Gear marketplace",\
  "Advertising",\
  "Community forums",\
  "Professional tournament tracking"\
]\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
excluded_features\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 This helps prevent the autonomous development system from building a monster.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Step 9: Define the MVP Scope\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Create a concise description of the smallest useful product.\
Answer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
What can a user do?\
\
What problem does it solve?\
\
What is intentionally missing?\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
The MVP allows an individual angler to create fishing\
trips and record catches with basic information,\
locations, and photos.\
\
The user can view their personal catch history.\
\
The MVP does not include social features, public maps,\
weather forecasting, community content, or advanced\
analytics.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Store:\
